// Multi-iteration 1D cellular automaton — the "Game of Life
// (multi-iter)" fixture from PRD §9 row 11.
//
// A 1D stencil applied to a circular (toroidal) grid of N cells, for
// ITERS generations:
//
//   grid[0][i]   = seed[i]                                       (t=0)
//   grid[t][i]   = grid[t-1][(i-1+N)%N]                          (t>=1)
//                + grid[t-1][i]
//                + grid[t-1][(i+1)%N]
//
// `step_or_seed(prev_l, prev_m, prev_r, seed_m, t)` does both:
// returns `seed_m` when `t == 0`, returns `prev_l + prev_m + prev_r`
// otherwise. Implementation in `kernels.rs`. The kernel-side branch
// on `t` is the same boundary-handling trick example 04-prefix-sum
// uses (`exclusive_add`, `block_scan` — see that file's header for
// the "Nuc has no conditionals; carry/boundary logic lives in Rust
// kernels" rationale).
//
// Single-assignment shape (PRD §6.2.1): each `data` symbol is the
// LHS of EXACTLY ONE Dataflow statement, globally. We use three
// symbols:
//   - `seed`   — loaded once via `load_input`.
//   - `grid`   — the full per-generation state, computed by ONE
//                rectangular-accumulator-style for-loop (matching
//                example 04's shape: codegen pre-inits `grid` to 0,
//                the loop body reads grid[t-1] and writes grid[t]).
//   - `result` — extracted from `grid[ITERS]` via a copy loop, then
//                saved via `save_output`.
//
// The single-Dataflow-on-`grid` constraint is why we cannot have a
// separate `for i { grid[0][i] <-- ident(seed[i]) }` seed-staging
// loop alongside the iteration loop — that would be two Dataflows
// on `grid` and the SSA check (algo/lower.rs, DoubleAssignment) would
// reject it. Folding the seed case into the iteration body via a
// kernel-side branch on `t` is the smallest change that preserves
// single-assignment.
//
// `grid` is indexed `grid[t][i]` where `t` is the generation index;
// the LHS writes `grid[t][i]` for `t in 0..ITERS+1`. For the
// prev-state read, `grid[(t+ITERS) % (ITERS+1)][...]` evaluates to
// `grid[ITERS][...]` at `t==0` (uninitialized cells, ignored by the
// kernel) and to `grid[t-1][...]` for `t>=1` (the cells written by
// the previous iteration). The modulo wrap is purely to keep the
// index expression IN-BOUNDS for `t==0` — the kernel discards
// whatever it reads there.
//
// What this example demonstrates
// ------------------------------
//   - The smallest fixture with a CROSS-ITERATION data dependency:
//     generation `t` reads cells of generation `t-1`. This is the
//     M4-capability surface PRD §9 row 11 calls out — multi-iteration
//     stencil, double-buffer.
//   - The SAME `prog.algo.nuc` compiles under a single-worker `naive`
//     schedule (smoke test, runs on every tier-1 backend) AND a
//     two-worker `pipelined` schedule (only the async-capable backend,
//     `pthreads-async`, can run it). The algorithm/schedule split
//     (PRD §2) survives the capability spread.
//   - That `pipeline=2 + buffer=2 + notify=event` on the
//     cross-iteration `grid` symbol is a meaningful capability test:
//     the buffer=2 is literally a double-buffer (two slots holding
//     consecutive generations in flight at steady state).
//
// What this example does NOT demonstrate
// --------------------------------------
//   - 2D stencil with halo regions. PRD §9 row 11 names "Game of
//     Life", which in its classical Conway form is a 2D 8-neighbour
//     rule with halo exchange — that is TASK-0117 / TASK-0126
//     territory and exceeds the current backend's distributed-halo
//     capability. The 1D wrap-around here is the smallest concrete
//     realisation of "iterated stencil" that the existing backends
//     can carry; see README §"Algorithm choice (option A vs B)" for
//     the discussion.
//   - Reduction, sort, or any non-deterministic-under-reorder
//     operation. The `step_or_seed` kernel is pure and three-way
//     associative; each `grid[t][i]` depends only on three cells of
//     `grid[t-1]` (or on `seed[i]` for `t==0`), so ordering across
//     `i` does not affect the output bits.
//   - Multi-stage pipelining of the loop body. Example 9 is the
//     two-stage producer/consumer fixture; this example is the
//     iterated-state fixture. Both are M4, exercising different axes
//     of the buffer/pipeline/event capability set.
//
// Numeric type: i32 throughout (PRD §13, docs/reference-impl-policy.md
// §5). All arithmetic uses `wrapping_add` in `kernels.rs` and the
// independent reference; the committed input + ITERS=8 generations
// stay inside the i32 range (worst-case growth is 3^8 ≈ 6561 from a
// 7-valued seed, well below i32::MAX) so wrap is never tripped in
// practice — the choice documents intent.

const N     : usize = 32;     // 32 cells per generation
const ITERS : usize = 8;      // 8 generations


// Whole-program input, full per-generation state, output. Each is
// assigned exactly once at the data-symbol level (PRD §6.2.1):
//   - `seed[i]`         once, by `seed <-- load_input()`.
//   - `grid[t][i]`      once per (t, i), by the iteration loop.
//   - `result[i]`       once per i, by the result-stage loop.
data seed   : i32[N];
data grid   : i32[ITERS+1][N];
data result : i32[N];


// Pure stencil step (with seed fallback for `t==0`). Scalar signature
// — the contract pass (TASK-0012) is scalar-only at present. The
// three `prev_*` args are the three-tap neighbours read from
// `grid[t-1]`; `seed_m` is the seed cell at index `i`; `t` is the
// generation index (0 means "use seed_m as the result"; t>=1 means
// "sum the three prev_* taps").
//
// The five-argument shape matches example 04-prefix-sum's
// `block_scan` (5 scalar args), `exclusive_add` (4 scalar args), and
// example 05-stencil's `blur3` (9 scalar args) — scalar kernel
// contracts that the M1-era contract pass already accepts.
kernel step_or_seed : (i32, i32, i32, i32, i32) -> i32 pure;

// Identity copy. Used to extract the final generation `grid[ITERS]`
// into a flat `result` array that the aggregate-typed `save_output`
// can consume. The staging loop adds N kernel calls of overhead over
// a hypothetical direct-sub-array I/O surface; that overhead is the
// price of staying inside TASK-0012's scalar contract pass at the
// current milestone.
kernel ident : (i32) -> i32 pure;

// I/O kernels. Effectful: ordering preserved, never duplicated. Same
// `Vec<i32>`-on-the-Rust-side convention as examples 01..07/09/13
// (TASK-0103 is the open question for aggregate-type matching).
kernel load_input  : ()       -> i32[N] effectful;
kernel save_output : (i32[N]) -> ()     effectful;


// Dataflow.
//
// 1. Load the seed.
seed <-- load_input();

// 2. Compute every generation. Single Dataflow on `grid` covers both
//    the seed-staging case (`t == 0`) and the iteration case
//    (`t >= 1`); `step_or_seed` branches on `t` to pick which to do.
//
//    `(t + ITERS) % (ITERS + 1)` is the previous-generation index:
//    - At `t == 0`, it evaluates to `ITERS` — reads `grid[ITERS]`
//      which is pre-initialised to 0 by codegen. The kernel ignores
//      those reads and returns `seed[i]`.
//    - At `t >= 1`, it evaluates to `t - 1` — reads the just-written
//      cells of the previous generation. The kernel returns the
//      three-tap sum.
//
//    This is the load-bearing cross-iteration data edge a pipelined
//    schedule streams as an async, buffered channel (`grid[t-1]` ->
//    `grid[t]`). Iterations of the outer `t` loop have a
//    read-after-write dependency on `grid` through this edge.
for t : 0 .. ITERS+1 {
for i : 0 .. N {
    grid[t][i] <-- step_or_seed(
        grid[(t + ITERS) % (ITERS + 1)][(i + N - 1) % N],
        grid[(t + ITERS) % (ITERS + 1)][i],
        grid[(t + ITERS) % (ITERS + 1)][(i + 1) % N],
        seed[i],
        t
    );
}}

// 3. Extract the final generation into a flat array for save_output.
//    Each `result[i]` is written exactly once.
for i : 0 .. N {
    result[i] <-- ident(grid[ITERS][i]);
}

// 4. Save.
save_output(result);
